sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: If a parallelogram has the same base with a triangle and is in the same parallels, then the parallelogram is double the triangle.
----
proof:

Let the parallelogram {ABCD} have the same base {BC} with the triangle {EBC}, and let it be in the same parallels {BC} and {AE}.

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="250" height="200" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="I.41 diagram" src="propI41.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // "Double" is the whole point, so the figure shows it rather than asserting
    // it: the diameter AC cuts ABCD into ABC and ACD, and ABC equals EBC by
    // I.37 — so all THREE triangles are equal, and two of them tile the
    // parallelogram. Colour says exactly that. ABC and EBC, the pair I.37 makes
    // equal, share cyan; ACD — the other half, which the proof reaches through
    // "the diameter bisects it" — is pink. The parallelogram is left as an
    // unfilled outline throughout, so what fills it is always its own halves.
    //
    // Fills are gated pairwise as in I.39/I.40: slide 4 shows ABC vs EBC (the
    // I.37 equality), slide 5 drops EBC so the ABC+ACD tiling reads clean (the
    // I.34 bisection), and slide 6 brings all three back for the conclusion.
    // ACD is ours — Joyce never names it — so it lives only in the figure and
    // in our captions, never in his prose.
    var CORE = ["A","B","C","D","E","ABCD","DE","BC","AE","BE","CE"];
    var BASE = CORE.concat(["BCE"]);
    var JOIN = BASE.concat(["AC"]);
    var EQ   = CORE.concat(["AC","ABC","BCE"]);
    var HALF = CORE.concat(["AC","ABC","ACD"]);
    var ALL  = CORE.concat(["AC","ABC","ACD","BCE"]);
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "I.41",
        elements: [
            "B;point;free;60,170",
            "C;point;free;130,170",
            "A;point;free;40,40",
            // The parallelogram stays an unfilled outline — its own halves are
            // what ever fills it. D is its fourth vertex.
            "ABCD;polygon;parallelogram;A,B,C",
            "D;point;vertex;ABCD,4",
            // E slides along AD produced, so the triangle shares base BC with
            // the parallelogram and sits in the same parallels.
            "E;point;lineSlider;A,D,210,40",
            "DE;line;connect;D,E",
            "AC;line;connect;A,C",
            // The triangle's two free sides, drawn in their own right so its
            // outline stands on slide 5 where its fill is gated off.
            "BE;line;connect;B,E",
            "CE;line;connect;C,E",
            // The shared base and the top parallel AE (= AD produced through
            // D to E): hover targets, already drawn by the figure.
            "BC;line;connect;B,C;0;0;0",
            "AE;line;connect;A,E;0;0;0",
            // Regions. ABC and EBC are the pair I.37 makes equal, so they share
            // cyan; ACD, the other half of the parallelogram, is pink.
            "BCE;polygon;triangle;B,C,E;0;0;0;cyan",
            "ABC;polygon;triangle;A,B,C;0;0;0;cyan",
            "ACD;polygon;triangle;A,C,D;0;0;0;pink"
        ],
        aliases: { "EBC": "BCE", "BEC": "BCE", "CB": "BC", "EA": "AE",
                   "EB": "BE", "EC": "CE", "CA": "AC", "ED": "DE" },
        initiallyHidden: ["ABC","ACD"],
        slides: [
            { text: "Let the parallelogram {ABCD} have the same base {BC} with the triangle {EBC}, and let it be in the same parallels {BC} and {AE}.",
              visible: BASE,
              highlighted: ["ABCD","BCE","BC"] },
            { text: "I say that the parallelogram {ABCD} is double the triangle {BEC}.",
              visible: BASE,
              highlighted: ["ABCD","BCE"] },
            { text: "Join {AC}.",
              visible: JOIN,
              highlighted: ["AC"],
              transition: { animations: [ { elem: "AC", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect } ] },
              justifications: [{ ref: "I.Post.1" }] },
            { text: "Then the triangle {ABC} equals the triangle {EBC}, for it is on the same base {BC} with it and in the same parallels {BC} and {AE}.",
              visible: EQ,
              highlighted: ["ABC","BCE","BC"],
              transition: { animations: [ { elem: "ABC", name: geomlib.A.Polygon.outlineAndFill } ] },
              justifications: [{ ref: "I.37" }] },
            { text: "But the parallelogram {ABCD} is double the triangle {ABC}, for the diameter {AC} bisects it.",
              visible: HALF,
              highlighted: ["ABC","ACD","AC"],
              transition: { animations: [ { elem: "ACD", name: geomlib.A.Polygon.outlineAndFill } ] },
              justifications: [{ ref: "I.34" }] },
            { text: "So the parallelogram {ABCD} is also double the triangle {EBC}.",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["ABCD","BCE"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "Q.E.D." }] }
        ],
        resolveJustification: function(ref) {
            return (window.eucrefs && window.eucrefs[ref]) || null;
        }
    });

    // Per-page citation lookup for the slideshow's resolveJustification.
    window.eucrefs = (window.eucrefs || {});
    Object.assign(window.eucrefs, {
        "I.Post.1": "/elements/books/bookI/postulates/post1/",
        "I.34":     "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI34/",
        "I.37":     "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI37/"
    });
</script>
</figure>

I say that the parallelogram {ABCD} is double the triangle {BEC}.

Join {AC}. [!just I.Post.1]

Then the triangle {ABC} equals the triangle {EBC}, for it is on the same base {BC} with it and in the same parallels {BC} and {AE}. [!just I.37]

But the parallelogram {ABCD} is double the triangle {ABC}, for the diameter {AC} bisects it, so that the parallelogram {ABCD} is also double the triangle {EBC}. [!just I.34]

Therefore *if a parallelogram has the same base with a triangle and is in the same parallels, then the parallelogram is double the triangle.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
<br clear="all">
----
guide:

This partially generalizes @I.34, that a parallelogram is twice the triangle by its diameter and two of its sides. A slightly more general statement would be that If a parallelogram has an equal base with a triangle and is in the same parallels, then the parallelogram is double the triangle.

#### Use of Proposition 41

This proposition is used in the next one, @I.47, @VI.1, and @X.38.
